// THE GUARD SWITCH, LEVEL 2 (spec 13.9 N10).  DETAIL wrangle, 64.
//   input 0  the DEFORM GATE's plan points - every piece the build planned
//   input 1  `pc_place_valid`'s output - every piece this branch will build
//   input 2  `pc_envelope` (level 1's verdict)
//
// Level 1 asked what the PARAMETERS and the SPLINE allow.  This asks the one
// question only the plan can answer: did the native chain end up able to build
// EVERY piece the build planned?
//
// ⚠️ AND IT ASKS IT BY COUNTING, NOT BY RE-DERIVING.  Every reason a piece
// leaves this branch is a blast - `pc_place_valid` drops what `pc_proto` and
// the gate declared unanswerable (a missing module, D99's band, 4.5's normal,
// a sliced piece, a span D31's frame transport could flip on) - so "nothing
// was dropped" is exactly `npoints(gate) == npoints(valid)`.  Two integers,
// O(1), and it cannot fall out of step with the blasts the way a second copy
// of their conditions would: adding a reason to drop a piece is automatically
// a reason to refuse the build.
//
// ⚠️ INPUT 1 IS `pc_place_valid` AND IT USED TO BE `pc_packed_only`, WHICH IS
// 13.9 N5's ONE-LINE SUMMARY.  While the deformed branch did not exist those
// two were the same set and the sentence above was true of either; N5 split
// them.  `pc_packed_only` and `pc_deformed_only` are now a PARTITION of
// `pc_place_valid`, not a drop and a remainder, and counting only the packed
// half would refuse every build with one bending panel in it - the exact
// refusal N5 exists to remove, measured at 1 237 ms of Python on 300 gently
// hilly streets.

int planned = npoints(0);
int built   = npoints(1);
int level1  = detail(2, "_native_ok");

i@_native_ok2 = (level1 && planned > 0 && planned == built);
i@_guard_planned = planned;
i@_guard_built = built;
